TCG's code buffer is reserved next to the helpers at load time, so a
helper call is a near call in every launch.

The aarch64 backend emits a helper call as one BL when the target is within
128 MiB, as ADRP+ADD+BLR within 4 GiB, and beyond that as MOVZ + three
MOVK + BLR; every host address a TB materialises (the indirect-jump helper,
exit constants) follows the same rule. The buffer is mmap(NULL, 1 GiB) at
TCG init, and on macOS/arm64 that landed at 0x3_0000_0000 -- 8 GiB from
the text -- in about a third of launches, whenever the guest RAM and the
libraries had already fragmented the space below the dyld shared cache.
Those launches ran helper-heavy guest code 35-45 % slower with a
byte-identical binary: x87 and SSE helpers at 0.55-0.65x, 7-Zip 0.89x, a
tight generated loop 1.00x (docs/22-tcg-evaluation.md §5.0, 2026-09-15:
found as "our tree with every switch off is 0.71x of stock", which was
this lottery, not the patches). Pristine QEMU has it too.

An mmap hint at TCG-init time is too late (the near windows are partly
occupied and macOS returns the next free 1 GiB gap, 8 GiB away). A
constructor runs when the image loads, before main() and before guest RAM
exists, and takes a PROT_NONE MAP_JIT reservation within 2 GiB of its own
image -- 1 GiB, or 512 or 256 MiB when the space above the image is
already fragmented (it usually is: six paused launches got 1 GiB five
times and 512 MiB once) -- -- the helpers live in the same image, whether that is
qemu-system-i386 or libqemu-embed inside the player; alloc_code_gen_buffer_anon
uses the reservation when the request is what it would have mapped
anyway (PROT_NONE, MAP_JIT), shrinking the buffer to the reservation when
that is smaller (a smaller buffer near beats a large one far), and falls
back to mmap(NULL) otherwise. QEMU_JIT_DEBUG=1 prints every try. No switch: it runs before the command line exists;
the A/B is a pristine build (build/qemu-stock), six ASLR-on launches each
in doc 22 §5.0. Darwin only: Linux/x86-64 has the same reach rules (CALL
rel32 = 2 GiB) but a different allocator; not measured there.

